(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-oxygen-delivery-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Oxygen Delivery Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with lung disease is being assessed for home oxygen. Her resting room-air saturation is 92%. Which is the next step in her qualification?","choices":["Order a complete blood count","Check her saturation with exertion","Stop: she does not qualify","Prescribe oxygen at 2 L/min now"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A resting saturation of 92% is above the 88% rest cutoff, so rest alone does not qualify her. Qualification is documented during rest, exertion and/or sleep, so the next step is to check her saturation with exertion (and sleep if indicated). — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with chronic lung disease has thick secretions she struggles to cough up during a severe exacerbation. Besides medication delivery, which listed rationale for a nebulizer applies?","choices":["Preventing carbon dioxide retention","Replacing her need for oxygen","Secretion clearance","Delivering a fixed oxygen fraction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her thick secretions that she cannot cough up in a severe exacerbation match secretion clearance, which is a listed rationale for a nebulizer along with delivering liquid medication directly to the airways. The nebulizer helps move the mucus. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 75-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is on a nasal cannula. Her breathing rate and depth change constantly, and her saturation swings with them. Which delivery system would hold her fraction of inspired oxygen steady?","choices":["An air-entrainment system","A non-rebreather mask","A partial rebreather mask","A simple oxygen mask"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A nasal cannula's fraction varies with respiratory rate, inspiratory flow and tidal volume, which is why her saturation swings as her breathing changes. An air-entrainment system gives a fixed fraction that does not fluctuate with patient demand, so it would hold her fraction steady. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 8, 14, 15"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a daytime saturation of 93%. Overnight oximetry shows his saturation falling to 87% for long stretches. What does he qualify for?","choices":["Continuous oxygen at rest","Nothing, since daytime is normal","Oxygen during exertion","Nocturnal oxygen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His daytime saturation of 93% is normal, but overnight it falls to 87%. The sleep criterion is a saturation of 88% or less with a drop of 5% or more from baseline: 87% is under 88%, and 93 minus 87 is a 6% drop. He qualifies for nocturnal oxygen. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man is brought in after running a generator in a closed garage. He has a headache and is confused; pulse oximetry reads 98%. Which treatment is indicated?","choices":["Breathing into a paper bag","Oxygen therapy","No oxygen, since saturation is normal","A nebulized bronchodilator"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After exposure to generator exhaust in a closed garage, his headache and confusion fit carbon monoxide poisoning, which is a listed indication for oxygen therapy. The normal reading of 98% does not remove that indication, so oxygen is the treatment. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with interstitial lung disease, pulmonary hypertension and a hematocrit of 52% has a resting room-air saturation of 89%. Which finding makes her qualify?","choices":["Her pulmonary hypertension","Her hematocrit of 52%","Her interstitial lung disease","Nothing; 89% does not qualify"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her resting saturation of 89% is just above the Group I cutoff of 88%, so she needs a comorbidity. Pulmonary hypertension is one of the listed qualifying findings, so it makes her qualify as Group II. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with bronchitis is placed on a simple mask at 6 to 7 L/min. About what fraction of inspired oxygen is he receiving?","choices":["28%","40%","50%","80%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A simple mask is a low flow device set at 5-10 L/min, and its fraction depends on the flow: 5-6 L/min gives 35%, 6-7 L/min gives 40%, and 7-8 L/min gives 50%. At 6 to 7 L/min he is receiving about 40%. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 13, 14, 15"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has mild hypoxia, saturation 87%, during a cold. A student suggests a non-rebreather mask at 15 L/min \"to be safe.\" Why should that be avoided?","choices":["It cannot raise his saturation","It causes vasoconstriction in the lungs","It is a fire hazard in hospitals","It is meant for severe hypoxia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His hypoxia is mild at 87%, and a non-rebreather mask is used in severely hypoxic patients who are still ventilating well, delivering 60-100% at 10-15 L/min. For mild hypoxia the nasal cannula is the most commonly used device, so the non-rebreather overshoots his need. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 11, 15"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with mild hypoxia, saturation 89%, had both nostrils packed for a severe nosebleed. She is comfortable otherwise. Which device suits her mild hypoxia with her nose blocked?","choices":["Nasal cannula at 2 L/min","Trach collar","Non-rebreather at 15 L/min","Simple mask"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both nostrils are packed, so a nasal cannula would not deliver. Her hypoxia is mild at 89%, and she is comfortable otherwise. A simple mask, a low flow device that covers the nose and mouth, can still deliver oxygen, at 5-10 L/min. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 13, 15, 19"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with chronic lung disease and a 60-year-old man with pneumonia are both on oxygen. The nurse asks what saturation target to use for each. Which principle applies?","choices":["Both should match room air","The target varies with the patient","Both should be kept at 100%","Both should be kept at 88%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Response to oxygen is judged by saturation that improves to a target that varies with the patient. Her chronic lung disease and his pneumonia are different situations, so one number does not fit both, and each target is set for the patient. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman who smoked for 40 years has an arterial oxygen pressure of 57 mm Hg at rest on room air and a hematocrit of 58%. Which recertification interval applies to her home oxygen?","choices":["Every 1 month","Every 3 months","Every 12 months","Every 24 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her arterial oxygen pressure of 57 mm Hg lies in the 56-59 range, above the Group I limit of 55 mm Hg, so she needs a comorbidity. Her hematocrit of 58% is above 56%, which is polycythemia. That puts her in Group II, which is recertified every 3 months. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is on a Venturi mask set at 28%. His saturation is still below his target, and he is comfortable and alert. Which adjustment raises his fraction precisely?","choices":["Switch to a non-rebreather at 15 L/min","Add a reservoir bag to the mask","Switch to a nasal cannula at 2 L/min","Change to a higher-fraction port"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A Venturi mask changes its fraction through different ports (24-50% when flow is adjusted from 4-10 L/min). He is stable and comfortable at 28% but below his target, so the precise adjustment is a higher-fraction port. It keeps the fraction fixed. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman is going home on oxygen tanks. Her son asks what to do with the tanks and how to store them. Which property of the tanks drives the storage and disposal teaching?","choices":["They hold a liquid fuel","They emit carbon monoxide","They spoil within a week","They hold compressed gas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen tanks contain compressed gas, and that property drives the teaching on proper storage and disposal. Her son should store the tanks as the teaching directs and not discard them as ordinary trash. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man on postoperative day 1 after a knee replacement has an oxygen saturation of 89% on room air. He is comfortable, speaking full sentences, with no distress. Which device is the usual first choice?","choices":["Nasal cannula at a low flow","Non-rebreather mask","Simple mask at 10 L/min","Venturi mask at 50%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. After knee replacement he is comfortable and speaking full sentences with a saturation of 89% and no distress, so his hypoxia sits at the mild end. The nasal cannula, a low flow device set at 1-6 L/min, is the usual first choice for mild hypoxia and gives 24-44%. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 13, 15, 16"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman develops severe hypoxia, saturation 79%, from pneumonia. She is alert, breathing at a steady rate with good chest rise, and her arterial carbon dioxide is normal. Which device delivers the high fraction she needs?","choices":["Non-rebreather mask","Nasal cannula","Simple mask","Bag-valve mask"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her saturation of 79% is severe hypoxia, yet she is alert, breathing steadily with good chest rise, so she is still ventilating well. The non-rebreather mask is the device for severely hypoxic patients who are still ventilating well, giving 60-100% at 10-15 L/min through its reservoir bag. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with pneumonia has an arterial blood gas before and after starting oxygen. After oxygen, his arterial oxygen pressure is higher, his carbon dioxide is unchanged, and his pH has returned toward normal. How is this response read?","choices":["Carbon dioxide retention","No response","A response if saturation reaches 100%","A good response"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A good response is an increase in arterial oxygen pressure without an increase in arterial carbon dioxide, plus correction of the pH. His oxygen pressure rose, his carbon dioxide stayed unchanged, and his pH moved back toward normal, which meets all three. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with diabetes and an acute myocardial infarction has an oxygen saturation of 87% on room air. Which is the right approach to oxygen?","choices":["Wait for an arterial blood gas first","Withhold oxygen because of her diabetes","Give oxygen, since saturation is below 91%","Withhold oxygen because of vasoconstriction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her saturation of 87% is below the 91% cutoff. Oxygen is held back in a myocardial infarction because of vasoconstriction, but only while the saturation is 91% or higher. She is hypoxic, so the benefit of correcting hypoxia wins and she should receive oxygen. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with severe pneumonia is on an aerosol mask from an air-entrainment nebulizer. The team wants to raise its fraction to 0.80. Why is that setting a concern?","choices":["It is not recommended above 0.50","It cannot go above 0.28","It is recommended above 0.50","It changes with his breathing rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A fixed-fraction air-entrainment nebulizer can deliver higher fractions, but setting above 0.50 is not recommended. His team wants 0.80, which is beyond that ceiling, so it is a concern even though his pneumonia is severe. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man on home oxygen for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease arrives with burns to his lips and nose after lighting a cigarette while wearing his cannula. Which hazard does this show?","choices":["Mucosal irritation","Vasoconstriction","Fire","Hypercapnia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A man on home oxygen lights a cigarette while wearing his cannula and burns his lips and nose. Oxygen is highly flammable, and fire is a listed hazard of oxygen therapy, which is why no smoking is taught for every home oxygen user. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 7, 9"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man on home oxygen at 5 L/min by nasal cannula with no humidifier complains of a sore, dry nose and occasional bleeding. Which change addresses the cause?","choices":["Increase the flow to 6 L/min","Apply a petroleum lip and nose balm","Switch to a non-rebreather mask","Add humidity to the oxygen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. He is on 5 L/min by nasal cannula with no humidifier, and has a sore, dry nose with bleeding. That is mucosal irritation. Humidity is used with cannula flow rates greater than 4 L/min, so adding humidity to the oxygen treats the cause. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 7, 9, 15"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has poor finger perfusion, and the pulse oximeter will not give a steady reading. His arterial oxygen pressure is 55 mm Hg on room air at rest. What is his home oxygen status?","choices":["Does not qualify at 55 mm Hg","Qualifies in Group I","Qualifies in Group II","Cannot qualify without oximetry"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Documentation can use an arterial blood gas or pulse oximetry. An arterial oxygen pressure of 55 mm Hg or less qualifies at Group I, and his is 55, so he qualifies without the oximeter. Group I is recertified at 12 months. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man's family turned his home nasal cannula up to 9 L/min because he felt short of breath. About what fraction of inspired oxygen does he receive at that setting?","choices":["44%","56%","60%","100%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A nasal cannula can be turned to any setting, but the fraction stops at 44% (reached at 6 L/min). At 9 L/min he gets no more than that, so the extra 3 liters add oxygen to his nose but not to his fraction. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 11, 14, 15"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with chronic lung disease is assessed for home oxygen before discharge. Her resting room-air saturation is exactly 88%, and she has no other findings. What is her qualification?","choices":["Group I, recertified at 12 months","Group II, recertified at 3 months","Not qualified without a comorbidity","Qualified for exertion oxygen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Group I qualifies at a resting room-air saturation of 88% or less, and hers is exactly 88%, which meets the cutoff. Group I patients are recertified at 12 months, and no comorbidity is needed. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man starting home oxygen asks why he must be so careful at home if oxygen is \"just air.\" Which property of oxygen underlies the safety teaching?","choices":["It is heavier than water","It turns into carbon monoxide","It is highly flammable","It is toxic to breathe for visitors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen is highly flammable, which is why home oxygen teaching includes no smoking in the home, staying away from heat sources, avoiding petroleum products and posting signs. Oxygen is not just air, and fire is a listed hazard. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with a long-term tracheostomy after head and neck cancer needs humidified supplemental oxygen at home. Which interface delivers air-entrainment nebulizer oxygen to his airway?","choices":["Open face mask","Trach collar","Nasal cannula","Simple mask"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He has a long-term tracheostomy and needs humidified oxygen delivered by an air-entrainment nebulizer. The trach collar is the air-entrainment nebulizer interface that fits over a tracheostomy, so it delivers to his airway directly. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man on home oxygen has a gas water heater with a pilot light in the hallway where he likes to sit. How far from it should his oxygen be kept?","choices":["At least 2 feet","At least 5 feet","At least 10 feet","At least 50 feet"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen teaching is to keep oxygen at least 10 feet from heat sources, which include pilot lights, electric appliances and candles. A gas water heater with a pilot light sits in the hallway where he sits, so his oxygen must be kept at least 10 feet away. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman using home oxygen keeps an electric space heater beside her recliner, where she sits with her tubing. Which teaching applies?","choices":["Lower her flow while the heater runs","Keep the heater 3 feet away","Keep oxygen at least 10 feet from the heater","A heater is safe because it has no flame"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen teaching is to keep oxygen at least 10 feet from heat sources, including electric appliances as well as pilot lights and candles. A space heater beside her recliner is a heat source, so she must keep her oxygen at least 10 feet away. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with interstitial lung disease has a resting room-air saturation of 93%. When he walks the hallway it falls to 85%, and with 2 L/min by nasal cannula it rises to 92% while walking. What does he qualify for?","choices":["Nothing, since rest is normal","Oxygen during sleep","Oxygen during exertion","Oxygen at rest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. At rest his saturation of 93% is above 88%, but walking drops it to 85%, at or below 88%, and with 2 L/min by cannula it rises to 92%. The exertion criterion is a saturation of 88% or less that improves when oxygen is given, so he qualifies for oxygen during exertion. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with influenza has mild to moderate respiratory distress and a saturation of 88%. She keeps mouth-breathing because her nose is congested. Which device suits her level of distress and delivers through both nose and mouth?","choices":["Non-rebreather mask","Trach collar","Simple mask","Nasal cannula"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. She has mild to moderate respiratory distress with a saturation of 88%, and with a congested nose she breathes through her mouth. A simple mask is a low flow device for mild to moderate distress that covers nose and mouth, so oxygen reaches her whichever way she breathes. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 13, 15, 19"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with bronchitis is on a nasal cannula at 6 L/min with a saturation of 89%. He now has mild to moderate respiratory distress but is ventilating well. Which step fits his severity?","choices":["Turn the cannula to 10 L/min","Go straight to a bag-valve mask","Stop oxygen and recheck in an hour","Change to a simple mask at 5 to 10 L/min"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. He has mild to moderate respiratory distress, and his saturation of 89% persists on a cannula at 6 L/min, near the cannula's limit of 44%. A simple mask, a low flow device set at 5-10 L/min, is the step for this severity. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 13"}]};
